  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:fieller-inversion",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Under i.i.d. sampling, finite second moments, full assignment support, and Fieller nondegeneracy, \\(P\\bigl((\\mu_t^\\star)_{t\\in\\mathcal B}\\in\\prod_{t\\in\\mathcal B}\\mathcal F_t\\bigr)\\ge1-\\alpha_0+o(1)\\). Hence \\(H(\\prod_{t\\in\\mathcal B}\\mathcal F_t)\\) covers \\(\\boldsymbol\\theta\\) with probability at least \\(1-\\alpha_0+o(1)\\); the set may be unbounded or disconnected.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\mu_t^\\star",
      "t",
      "\\mathcal B",
      "\\mathcal F_t",
      "\\alpha_0",
      "H",
      "\\boldsymbol\\theta"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:full-support-randomization",
      "ass:iid-sampling",
      "ass:second-moment",
      "ass:fieller-nondegeneracy",
      "def:estimators",
      "def:joint-influence",
      "def:fieller-sets",
      "thm:signed-identification",
      "lem:finite-cell-asymptotics"
    ],
    "justification": "Test inversion remains informative about identification weakness because it is allowed to return unbounded sets.",
    "gap": "Existing factorial-IV work treats scalar Fieller intervals; the contribution here is the explicit Bonferroni product and Walsh image for the dependent eight-ratio system, not uniform weak-share adaptation.",
    "consumer": "Applied analysts obtain a simultaneous fallback when the estimated perfect-complier share is too small for the Wald ellipsoid.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix a bundle \\(t\\) and put \\(u=\\mu_t^\\star\\).  Signed identification gives the zero population moment\n\\[\nm_t-u p_t=p^\\star\\mu_t^\\star-\\mu_t^\\star p^\\star=0. \\tag{35}\n\\]\nThe finite-cell asymptotic lemma and linearity give\n\\[\n\\sqrt n\\bigl[(\\widehat m_t-u\\widehat p_t)-(m_t-u p_t)\\bigr]\n=n^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\n\\bigl(\\phi_{m,t}(O_i)-u\\phi_{p,t}(O_i)\\bigr)+o_P(1). \\tag{36}\n\\]\nThe assumed variance is strictly positive at this fixed \\(u\\), and finite second moments hold by the outcome moment assumption and bounded receipt indicators.  Thus the consistent plug-in variance in the definition of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) and the studentized conclusion of the cited lemma imply\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt n(\\widehat m_t-u\\widehat p_t)}\n{\\widehat{\\operatorname{Var}}(\\phi_{m,t}-u\\phi_{p,t})^{1/2}}\n\\Rightarrow N(0,1). \\tag{37}\n\\]\nSince \\(z_{1-\\alpha_0/16}\\) is the two-sided normal critical value with tail probability \\(\\alpha_0/8\\), (37) and the definition of \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) give\n\\[\nP(\\mu_t^\\star\\in\\mathcal F_t)=1-\\frac{\\alpha_0}{8}+o(1). \\tag{38}\n\\]\nThere are exactly eight bundles.  The union bound, which requires no independence among the eight statistics, yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\left((\\mu_t^\\star)_{t\\in\\mathcal B}\n \\in\\prod_{t\\in\\mathcal B}\\mathcal F_t\\right)\n&\\ge1-\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal B}P(\\mu_t^\\star\\notin\\mathcal F_t)\\\\\n&\\ge1-\\alpha_0+o(1).\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{39}\n\\]\nWhenever the event in (39) occurs, the identity \\(\\boldsymbol\\theta=H\\boldsymbol\\mu^\\star\\) implies\n\\[\n\\boldsymbol\\theta\\in H\\left(\\prod_{t\\in\\mathcal B}\\mathcal F_t\\right),\n\\]\nwhich proves the simultaneous Walsh coverage claim.\n\nFor fixed data, the defining Fieller inequality is a quadratic inequality in \\(u\\), because both \\((\\widehat m_t-u\\widehat p_t)^2\\) and the empirical variance of \\(\\phi_{m,t}-u\\phi_{p,t}\\) are quadratic polynomials in \\(u\\).  Its leading coefficient need not be positive; consequently its solution can be a bounded interval, a half-line, all of \\(\\mathbb R\\), or the union of two rays, the last case being disconnected.  Products of such sets can therefore be unbounded or disconnected, and because every column of \\(H\\) is nonzero, configurations with separated components can retain that disconnection after the Walsh map.  Hence neither boundedness nor connectedness is asserted for the simultaneous construction.  The argument is pointwise for a fixed law with \\(p^\\star>0\\) and positive studentizing variances; it makes no uniform claim for sequences with \\(p^\\star\\) vanishing.",
